<p>It&#8217;s a real grab bag of apps in this week&#8217;s report, but that seems wholly appropriate somehow. After all, most of us turn to our Macs for a variety of reasons, so why not a selection of apps that speed up image processing, give you new controls over your tunes or help you keep a cleaner, better organized desktop? Oh, and there&#8217;s an iOS app in there, too, for the next time the travel bug bites you.</p>
<p>So why not reach in and see if any of the apps below grab you? And if there&#8217;s something we haven&#8217;t covered, drop us a line with the details at <code>apps@sixcolors.com</code>. We can&#8217;t respond individually, but rest assured that we take a look at every suggestion we get.</p>
<h2>Freshly Squeezed</h2>
<p>Each week I produce a <a href="https://get-your-phil-newsletter-ccdfef.beehiiv.com">weekly newsletter</a> where I write about whatever&#8217;s on my mind — you should subscribe! — and one of the big production tasks is to take the images I&#8217;ve shot and convert them into 16:9 JPEGs cropped down to 2000 x 1125. It&#8217;s a job that would definitely benefit from batch processing, which is where an app like <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/freshly-squeezed-batch-resize/id6752724547?mt=12">Freshly Squeezed</a> comes into play.</p>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/freshlysqueezed1-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="A Mac image-cropping app listing five HEIC photos ready to process. Export Settings show a 16:9 aspect ratio, JPEG format, 80% quality, EXIF retention unchecked, filename IMG_5861-16x9-autoxauto.jpg, a Drop Folder Run preset, and a Crop &amp; Export button." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>Freshly Squeezed handles the jobs you&#8217;d expect from a batch image processor: It can resize, crop, compress and convert images to different files, all while retaining your originals. I&#8217;ve set up the app so that I can drop photos into it and get the right cropping and conversion, with Freshly Squeezed giving me the option to individually tweak crops if I want.</p>
<p>The app&#8217;s showcase feature is called Drop Folder, which you set up when you need to perform the same image processing over and over again. Once you&#8217;ve set up the parameters, just drop images into the folder, and Freshly Squeezed handles the rest.</p>
<p>You can download Freshly Squeezed onto your Mac for $7. A 7-day trial lets you try things out for free.</p>
<h2>NepTunes</h2>
<figure class="pull-right"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/neptunes-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="NepTunes music player on Mac showing Buddy Guy's album &quot;A Man and the Blues&quot; at 0:58. A menu is open with Similar Artists highlighted, listing Buddy Guy &amp; Junior Wells, Albert King, B.B. King, Junior Wells, and Taj Mahal." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>For another way to control music playback on your Mac, turn to <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/neptunes-widget-last-fm/id1006739057?mt=12">NepTunes 4</a>, which works with both Apple Music and Spotify. In fact, the Pro version of the app even lets you toggle between the two music services.</p>
<p>At its most basic, NepTunes lives in your menu bar, where it shows you whatever song is currently playing along with playback controls. You can also customize NepTunes to display additional info — I&#8217;ve got the app showing recently played songs, and if you link it to your Last.fm profile, you can see stats affiliated with that account. From the NepTunes interface, you can look up similar artists, while Pro users can find similar tracks to what&#8217;s playing. And all NepTunes users can set up hotkeys to control different playback features.</p>
<p>I keep mentioning the Pro version, which is a $10/year subscription. Besides the aforementioned features, Pro users can display their top albums in a grid. They can also download desktop widgets in various styles, such as one that displays the album you&#8217;re listening to like it&#8217;s a CD in a jewel case. You can peruse the <a href="https://neptunesmac.app/widgets/">15 available widgets</a> to get a flavor for NepTunes&#8217; visual flair.</p>
<p>The Pro version comes with a 14-day free trial. Otherwise, NepTunes is a free download.</p>
<h2>Tidy</h2>
<p>It&#8217;s an occupational hazard that I have a lot of screenshots piled up around my Mac with names like <strong>Screenshot 2024-07-09 at 10.05.32 AM</strong> that tell me nothing about what&#8217;s actually contained in the screenshot. I imagine you might have the same problem with IMG files or PDFs or any file with an inscrutable collection of letters and numbers as its only means of identification.</p>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tidy-noshadow-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="A macOS 'Review names' window listing files with current and proposed names, like Screenshot 2026-10-01 becoming wish-toster.png. 6 of 40 are selected. Buttons at the bottom read Close and Rename (6); a note says files are renamed in place and can be undone." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="https://tidymacapp.com">Tidy</a> is the answer to our organizational prayers. This $9 macOS download takes the screenshots, scans and other oddly named files from your Downloads folder, takes a look at them using its own built-in OCR feature and proposes readable names to help you better identify what the file actually contains. You approve the names, so nothing&#8217;s forced upon you, and Tidy only generates names using what it sees, so there are no hallucinations.</p>
<p>Even so, Tidy occasionally misses the mark. For example, a screenshot of the NepTunes app from up above got renamed &#8220;dutton-toster&#8221; because the font for singer Sutton Foster&#8217;s name proved too tricky to decipher. I was able to reject that suggestion, though, so no harm, no foul.</p>
<p>More often, Tidy proved to be on the money, as a photo of the ferris wheel looming over Disney&#8217;s California Adventure was retitled &#8220;blue-sky-land-amusement-park&#8221; — a much more decipherable moniker than <strong>IMG_435.HEIC</strong>.</p>
<h2>Appricio</h2>
<figure class="pull-right"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/appricio-framed-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="iPhone screenshot of the Appricio app showing the headline &quot;Sunny Thursday lunch in Alameda.&quot; Text recommends the Ferry Plaza Farmers Market, a 25 min drive. A Top Pick, Must-See photo shows the Ferry Building clock tower and market tents." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/appricio-be-a-local-anywhere/id6772486086">Appricio</a> looks to be an indispensable travel companion by taking advantage of your iPhone&#8217;s location awareness. Fire up Appricio in a city included in one of the iOS app&#8217;s guides, and it can tell you what to do based on where you are, what the weather is and preferences shared in the settings.</p>
<p>You won&#8217;t just get a list of places to visit with Appricio, though. It&#8217;s a curated guide that makes specific suggestions, with a bias toward taking you off the beaten path (at least when you&#8217;re amenable to that). For example, Appricio detected that I&#8217;m near San Francisco and that the weather&#8217;s nice right now, so it steered me away from Pier 39 — which it dismissed as a tourist trap — and guided me to a day in Golden Gate Park instead.</p>
<p>Appricio would be a fine app if it just helped you in the moment, but it helps you plan trips, too. That feature is somewhat limited by the fact that <a href="https://appricio.com/cities">the app only covers 50 cities</a> at the moment, though I was able to do web searches for an upcoming visit to San Diego (currently not among the included cities) without the benefit of Appricio&#8217;s curation.</p>
<p>The app is free, though a $30/year subscription gives you a dozen more cities, a multi-day planning feature in which Appricio helps build your trip and an events calendar, among other bonuses.</p>
<h2>Droppy</h2>
<figure class="pull-right"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/droppy1-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Dark Droppy app panel showing two clipboard items: a link, https://getdroppy.app/, from Safari 5s ago, and text &quot;Tidy macOS cleaning app...&quot; from BBEdit 17s ago with star, pin, and trash buttons. Home, inbox, and grid icons appear below." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>If you&#8217;ve looked at the notched display on your MacBook and thought, &#8220;I wish this worked more like the Dynamic Island on my iPhone,&#8221; <a href="https://getdroppy.app">Droppy</a> is here to answer the call. At its most basic, this app lets you drag documents and files up toward the notch, where they can be stored for easy access until you find a more permanent home for them. But Droppy&#8217;s feature set doesn&#8217;t end there.</p>
<p>Droppy provides access to other features, like music playback controls, a clipboard that keeps the history of things you copy and paste and a five-hour weather forecast. Additional widgets — or Droplets, as the app dubs them — can be added to handle things like editing screenshots, file conversion, transcription and more.</p>
<p>While the app complements notch-equipped machines, it brings those Dynamic Island capabilities to other Macs as well. Droppy is free to try for three days; afterwards, a $10 payment unlocks the app for up to two Macs.</p>
